' ============================================================================
'  IMPLEMENTATION NOTES
' ============================================================================
note as N_rules
  <b>Business rules enforced in the database</b>
  • <b>Single role:</b> every authenticated user is an analyst (row in <i>analysts</i>,
    created by trigger on auth.users). No admin, no user management.
  • <b>No review periods:</b> analysts work on the global transaction list or on the
    customer view (transactions of all the customer's accounts in a date range).
  • <b>One current label per transaction</b> (unique transaction_id). Any analyst can
    create or edit it; last write wins (<i>version</i> is incremented). The full history
    lives in <i>audit_log</i> (entity = 'labels', transaction_id).
  • <b>Labeling status:</b> <i>transactions.is_labeled</i> is maintained by a trigger on
    labels, so the "Unlabeled / Labeled" filter and progress counters are cheap.
  • <b>Single label:</b> RPC <i>save_label(transaction_id, ...)</i> upserts the label and its
    fraud types atomically; analyst_id and updated_at are set by the database.
  • <b>Bulk labeling:</b> RPC <i>label_transactions(ids[], ...)</i> applies one label to
    many transactions in a single transaction and returns how many were overwritten.
  • <b>Shared alert inbox:</b> RPC <i>resolve_alert(alert_id, action, ...)</i> locks the row
    (<i>SELECT ... FOR UPDATE</i>) and fails if status <> 'pending', so the first analyst
    wins. It creates/replaces the label of each alert transaction (source = 'alert'):
    confirm → fraud + suggested type; reject → not_fraud; reclassify → fraud + other type.
  • <b>Fixed taxonomy:</b> fraud_types, confidence_levels, severity_levels,
    severity_thresholds and exchange_rates are loaded by the seed; the app only reads them.
  • <b>Audit:</b> AFTER INSERT/UPDATE triggers on labels and ai_alerts write to
    audit_log. No UPDATE/DELETE grants for <i>authenticated</i>.
  • <b>Notifications:</b> AFTER INSERT trigger on ai_alerts creates one
    <i>alert_new</i> notification per analyst (per-analyst read state).
end note

note as N_supabase
  <b>Supabase</b>
  • <b>RLS</b> on every table in <i>public</i>: any authenticated analyst can read all
    banking data, alerts and labels (no masking, no per-customer restriction).
    Writes only on labels / label_fraud_types (and alerts through <i>resolve_alert()</i>);
    notifications: analyst_id = auth.uid().
  • <b>Realtime</b> enabled on: transactions (is_labeled), labels, ai_alerts,
    notifications. Connection status shown in the app.
  • <b>Read views</b>: v_transactions_list (with alert score, label status and
    location for the timeline and map),
    v_customer_label_progress, v_labeling_progress, v_fraud_stats (by type and
    currency), v_ai_performance (suggested vs. final type), v_stats_transactions
    (date, channel, country and amount in COP per transaction),
    v_account_balance_history.
    Out of scope: v_transfer_edges (graph), precision by score range.
  • <b>Statistics</b>: RPCs <i>fraud_stats_kpis</i>, <i>fraud_over_time</i>,
    <i>fraud_grouped(by)</i> and <i>ai_performance</i>, all filtered by date range and
    country; the committed amount is converted to COP with exchange_rates.
  • <b>Global search</b>: RPC <i>global_search(q)</i> over customers.search_vector,
    accounts.last4 and transactions.code.
  • <b>PDF reports</b> are generated on the client and saved locally (no Storage).
  • <b>Key indexes</b>: transactions(source_account_id, occurred_at),
    transactions(destination_account_id, occurred_at), transactions(is_labeled, occurred_at),
    ai_alerts(status, score DESC), security_events(customer_id, occurred_at),
    audit_log(entity, entity_id), audit_log(transaction_id),
    notifications(analyst_id) WHERE read_at IS NULL, pg_trgm on names.
end note

N_rules -[hidden]- N_supabase

@enduml
